AB  - Smart grids (SG) are advanced cyber-physical systems responsible for optimising power delivery across generation, transmission, and distribution infrastructure through real-time integration of sensors, control centres, and Internet of Things (IoT) devices. Advancements in communication technologies and artificial intelligence (AI) have significantly enhanced grid performance; however, they have simultaneously introduced critical vulnerabilities that adversaries exploit through Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS) attacks targeting grid availability. Although several surveys have studied SG cybersecurity, DDoS threats still require a more focused analysis that connects protocol vulnerabilities, threat modelling, detection methods, and cyber-physical impact. This study presents a PRISMA-compliant review of publications published between 2019 and June 2026, surveying DDoS attack characteristics, protocol-level infrastructure vulnerabilities, and formal threat-framework mappings. It evaluates both model-driven and data-driven detection methods across standardised benchmark datasets, and links power-system stability metrics, including the Rate of Change of Frequency, Rate of Change of Voltage, and Area Control Error, with cybersecurity evaluation to examine physical-layer consequences. Contemporary dataset and detection limitations are critically examined, and a structured future research roadmap is proposed. Overall, this survey provides a focused and operationally grounded reference for researchers and practitioners advancing DDoS resilience in next-generation critical infrastructure.
