KW  - Adversarial attacks
KW  - Diffusion models
KW  - Prompt embedding optimization
KW  - Controllable image editing
AB  - Deep neural networks remain vulnerable not only to small pixel-space perturbations but also to realistic semantic changes. Although unrestricted attacks move beyond Lp-norm noise, existing methods often optimize implicit latent variables or hand-crafted transformation parameters, which limit user control over the generated scene variation. To tackle this problem, we propose StylePromptAdv (SPA), a style-controllable unrestricted attack framework that turns text-guided image editing into a controllable adversarial generation process. Instead of perturbing pixels or diffusion latents, SPA optimizes the prompt embedding of a frozen diffusion editor, allowing adversarial cues to be embedded into user-specified edits. The framework couples a reference editing trajectory with an adversarial trajectory and optimizes only the textual condition, preserving the generative prior while maintaining semantic steerability. To make this optimization effective and stable, we introduce a Unified Exponential Margin Loss for architecture-agnostic attack supervision, a Perceptual Trajectory Alignment Loss that regularizes intermediate clean-state estimates along the denoising process, and a Prompt Consistency Loss that limits semantic drift. Extensive experiments on an ImageNet-compatible benchmark show that SPA achieves strong attack performance across convolutional and transformer-based classifiers while producing natural, instruction-faithful adversarial edits with favorable perceptual quality. The attack also remains effective under diffusion-based purification, revealing a persistent vulnerability of vision models to controllable, language-guided semantic perturbations.
ER  - 

