KW  - Cloud API recommendation
KW  - Poisoning attacks
KW  - Diffusion model
KW  - Surrogate model
AB  - QoS-aware cloud API recommender systems (QARS) play a vital role in addressing API overload, mitigating functional redundancy, and ensuring high-quality API selection. Since QARSs directly influence exposure and revenue, they are increasingly vulnerable to poisoning attacks. Traditional methods, however, often suffer from training instability and a lack of real-time feedback from the target system, failing to balance stealthiness and disruptiveness. To bridge this gap, we propose S-Diffusion, a novel poisoning attack method grounded in a surrogate diffusion model, following an “attack to promote defense” paradigm. S-Diffusion effectively balances stealthiness and disruptiveness, generating high-quality fake users to achieve targeted attack goals. Specifically, S-Diffusion first employs an autoencoder that learns compact behavioral representations from real users, which guide the forward diffusion process in modeling the transition from order to disorder. The reverse process then gradually denoises and reconstructs fake users that mimic true user behavior. Furthermore, a surrogate model evaluates the impact of these injections, and the resulting attack loss jointly optimizes the autoencoder and diffusion process. Extensive experiments on real-world datasets demonstrate that S-Diffusion outperforms state-of-the-art attack methods in both effectiveness and stealth. By exposing critical vulnerabilities, this work highlights the urgent need for more robust QoS-aware cloud API recommender systems.
ER  - 

