AB  - Tool-augmented agent frameworks expose routing, tool calls, shared state, and execution logs, but a recorded run does not by itself establish that permissions, budgets, provenance, and audit obligations were respected. We present a certification architecture for agent swarms in which coalgebra models observable state-based execution and a Subexponential Linear Logic-labelled ledger classifies reusable and consumable evidence. The contribution is the connection between these layers: certification filters raw executions into those satisfying graph, permission, budget, append-only memory, provenance, audit-freshness, and answer-support conditions. For the implemented six-rule fragment, certified finite paths form a prefix-closed sublanguage of raw behaviour; local rule soundness yields finite-run resource preservation. We implement an indexed, transactional trace checker for that certificate language, along with an adapter that captures native version-2 streams from three deterministic LangGraph 1.2.11 StateGraph workflows without a language model or commercial API. The checker matched all expectations in a 19-trace conformance suite, accepting five positive traces and rejecting fourteen negative controls. It accepted all three unmodified framework executions and rejected all 42 controlled mutations, with one expected violation code at the mutated step in each case. A scaling sanity check covered traces from 10 to 10,000 events with seven measured repetitions per size. These results establish executable trace certification and framework-integration feasibility for the studied deterministic setting; they do not establish factual correctness, adversarial security, or production-scale generality.
ER  - 

