AB  - UAV swarms are evolving into highly interconnected cyber-physical systems that integrate communication, control, and artificial intelligence to enable collective autonomy in dynamic and adversarial environments. While extensive survey literature exists on UAV communications, swarm intelligence, and AI-driven security, these works predominantly examine these dimensions in isolation, resulting in fragmented perspectives that fail to capture how security emerges from their joint interaction. In particular, existing surveys do not systematically analyze how communication constraints fundamentally shape the integration of optimization, control, and learning mechanisms in secure swarm operations. This paper addresses this gap by presenting a communication-centric, cross-layer synthesis of secure UAV swarm systems through a systematic analysis of over 100 peer-reviewed studies spanning 2010–2026. Unlike prior surveys, we explicitly position communication as the structural constraint governing the feasibility, scalability, and resilience of swarm intelligence. We introduce a novel Optimization–AI Coupling Framework, which characterizes the integration of learning and control across four coupling regimes (None, Sequential, Feedback, Tight), and systematically links them to practical communication modalities, including onboard-only architectures, FANETs, and 5G/6G-enabled Internet-of-Drones infrastructures. In addition, we propose an eight-dimensional swarm-security pipeline that unifies sensing, communication, coordination, control, defense, and governance within a single operational model. Through this framework, we move beyond descriptive survey methodology to provide analytical insights into how communication latency, reliability, and topology constraints influence guidance laws, model predictive control, federated learning, intrusion detection, and trust mechanisms in adversarial settings. A longitudinal analysis of case studies demonstrates the evolution from isolated control-driven UAV systems to fully networked, learning-enabled swarm architectures. Finally, we identify critical open challenges, including transient instability under adaptive adversaries, semantic-level security leakage, and multi-swarm coexistence, establishing a forward-looking research agenda for communication-aware, resilient swarm systems. This work differentiates itself from existing surveys by providing a unified, communication-conditioned framework that explicitly connects AI, optimization, and security across swarm communication architectures.
ER  - 

