AB  - Federated Learning (FL) is a transformative paradigm in decentralized Machine Learning (ML) introduced by Google in 2016. It tackles major challenges in decentralized ML, especially in big data environments, such as privacy, scalability, and heterogeneity. This paper reviews the fundamentals of FL, including its advantages in privacy-sensitive environments and diverse applications such as healthcare, Internet of Things (IoT), and smart cities. It also examines major challenges such as communication overhead, computation costs, and heterogeneity. A taxonomy is introduced to categorize FL settings based on data distribution, client participation, communication strategies, and aggregation methods. State-of-the-art studies and frameworks are analyzed in terms of their efficiency, privacy, and performance. This paper further explores context-aware client collaboration strategies for optimizing FL and tackling its challenges to some extent. Using geographical information is proposed to improve efficiency by grouping clients within the same region. Curriculum learning is suggested to prioritize clients with simpler data in early rounds to build a strong baseline and progressively incorporate clients with more complex data. Clustering clients based on data similarity is introduced to improve performance and reduce conflicts during aggregation. The use of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) for topology optimization is explored to dynamically adjust communication patterns by considering client attributes. The potentials of these strategies are also discussed according to the proposed taxonomy. Finally, open challenges and future directions are outlined as a potential research roadmap in the FL area.
