AB  - The convergence of the Internet of Things (IoT) and Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS) has transformed critical infrastructure into highly interdependent environments, where localized compromises can propagate into systemic cyber-physical threats. Although graph-based security analytics are well suited to capture the relational dependencies underlying such threats, the current literature remains fragmented, making it difficult to understand the relationship between system representation, analytical methods, and operational security goals. This study addresses this gap through a systematic literature review structured by a three-axis taxonomy that classifies prior work by graph abstraction level (what is modeled), analytical technique (how it is analyzed), and security objective (why it is done). Our analysis shows that existing research heavily focuses on graph neural network-based detection using network connectivity and provenance graphs, whereas higher-level abstractions, predictive security objectives, and resilience-oriented designs remain comparatively underexplored. We also identified major practical and methodological limitations, particularly in terms of benchmark realism, explainability, and deployment scalability. To address these gaps, we propose a forward-looking research roadmap centered on physics-aware hybrid learning, trustworthy causal reasoning, explainable by design, federated graph intelligence, adversarially robust, and human-centered security analytics. The resulting framework provides researchers and security professionals with structured guidance for advancing graph intelligence from reactive detection toward more context-aware and resilient IoT/CPS security solutions.
