AB  - Group recommendation aims to integrate members’ preferences for consensus-aligned recommendations. However, conformity bias often leads to a “pseudo-consensus” where active members dominate the aggregation process, suppressing the latent preferences of inactive members. To address this, we propose VAPrompt, a dual-branch collaborative framework that unifies member-level variational debiasing and group-level prompting consensus. Specifically, the member-level variational debiasing branch utilizes a variational hypergraph encoder and an efficient variational attention mechanism to capture member preference diversity and prevent active members from dominating the group representation. Furthermore, the group-level prompting consensus branch introduces a heterogeneity-aware prompt to guide a multi-view gating fusion mechanism, dynamically balancing contributions from different views to focus on the intersection of members’ real intentions. We evaluate the proposed method on four real-world datasets: Mafengwo, Mafengwo-S, Weeplaces-S, and CAMRa2011. Extensive experiments demonstrate that VAPrompt outperforms state-of-the-art baselines across all evaluation metrics. In particular, compared with the best baseline, VAPrompt achieves average improvements on the Weeplaces-S dataset, with gains of 18.26% in HR@10 and 16.84% in NDCG@10. These results validate the effectiveness of the proposed framework in mitigating conformity bias and improving group recommendation accuracy. The source code is available at github11https://anonymous.4open.science/r/VAPrompt-main-43DB/..
