AB  - The electricity-hydrogen integrated energy system (EH-IES) is increasingly recognized as a critical pathway for deep decarbonization. Nevertheless, prevailing reviews generally examine physical design, operational strategies, and market governance separately, leaving their cross-layer interdependencies insufficiently synthesized. The large-scale deployment of EH-IES necessitates the coordinated development of physical and information infrastructures, as well as the appropriate market, regulatory and social conditions. To address this gap, this review examines EH-IES from the physical-informational-social (PIS) perspective, with emphasis on the interactions among physical infrastructure, informational coordination, and social mechanisms. Through a structured synthesis of the selected literature, the physical layer compares hydrogen production, storage, transportation, and re-electrification technologies, together with their multi-timescale coupling constraints. The informational layer synthesizes artificial intelligence (AI)-driven sensing and state estimation, digital twin (DT)-enabled coordination, and cyber-physical security. The social layer examines institutional regulation, multi-energy aggregator (MEA) operation, market incentives, demand response, and social acceptance. Based on the PIS architecture, this review synthesizes a 4E assessment framework that covers energy conversion and renewable energy utilization; exergy destruction and flow tracing; system costs and profitability; and life-cycle emissions and their associated social value. It further classifies cross-layer operational strategies into physical-informational, informational-social, and physical-social coordination. The principal deployment challenges include infrastructure scalability, bankable multi-value markets, audit-grade digitalization, cyber-resilient operation, system-level certification, and social licensing. A staged research agenda is outlined from diagnosable pilots to interoperable regional hubs and climate-resilient PIS infrastructure.
ER  - 

