T1  - Red teaming generative artificial intelligence in nursing education: A risk-based audit
AU  - De Gagne, Jennie C.
AU  - Hwang, Hyeyoung
AU  - Gris, Ivan
JO  - Journal of Nursing Regulation
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/10/12/
SN  - 2155-8256
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnr.2026.09.002
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2155825626001171
KW  - Generative artificial intelligence
KW  - Nursing education
KW  - Academic integrity
KW  - AI governance
KW  - Nursing regulation
AB  - Background
The use of generative artificial intelligence (AI) tools in nursing education without discipline-specific evaluation can create risks in admissions gatekeeping, clinical knowledge verification, and academic integrity assessment.
Purpose
This red-team risk-identification audit evaluated Copilot (Microsoft Corporation) on tasks representative of nursing education practice.
Methods
The research team deliberately constructed 30 high-risk test cases that spanned three pillars (admissions bias, factual and clinical accuracy, academic integrity) plus one supplementary test. The study was designed to identify plausible AI failure modes rather than to solicit AI performance rates for statistical generalization. Test materials were generated using Claude Sonnet 4.6 (Anthropic) or were human written, and all materials were submitted to Copilot in isolated single-turn interactions. In the academic integrity generation tests, Copilot's own output was the object of evaluation. Responses were classified using a 6-category taxonomy and rated by the authors on a 5-point severity scale anchored to discipline-specific consequences.
Results
At least one issue was identified in every test case, as expected for tests designed to elicit failure. Sociodemographic bias was the most frequent category (n = 11) and appeared across all three pillars. Mean severity on a 5-point scale (1 = low severity to 5 = critical severity) was 3.00 overall and 3.30 for Pillar 1, 3.09 for Pillar 3, and 2.60 for Pillar 2. Fluent, professionally toned responses obscured underlying issues, including one double misclassification (P3T8) that was rated at critical severity.
Conclusion
The findings revealed a trust paradox: the system performed most fluently where accuracy, fairness, and accountability were most critical, thus supporting concerns about unexamined high-stakes use rather than conclusions about overall performance. Detection-based integrity strategies were insufficient, supporting disclosure-based approaches grounded in professional accountability. Regulatory bodies can require AI oversight within existing program approval and standards processes, while institutions can define tiered criteria regarding acceptable use. Faculty and students must be prepared to question, verify, and govern AI use.
