AB  - The rapid adoption of LLM-powered multi-agent systems has brought users with diverse AI expertisefrom software developers to domain experts-into the process of building and maintaining complex agentic workflows. Yet when these distributed systems fail, their non-deterministic and opaque nature makes it difficult for users to observe, attribute, and correct errors. To understand practitioners needs, we conducted formative interviews with 12 practitioners spanning AI developers who build agentic systems to domain experts who utilize them, surfacing five key challenge themes and four design goals for explainability tools. Building on these insights, we designed XAgen, an explainability interface that supports failure detection and correction through three capabilities: log visualization for glanceable workflow understanding, human-in-the-loop feedback to capture expert judgment, and automatic error identification via an LLM-as-a-judge, which reached 95% accuracy against author-verified labels. In a usability evaluation with 8 participants, XAgen was rated significantly higher than a state-of-the-art observability baseline across all four dimensionsunderstanding, usability, helpfulness, and satisfaction-with median scores of 67 out of 7 (all p<0.05). Log visualization received the highest helpfulness rating (median = 5/5, p<0.01), followed by automatic error identification (median = 4.5/5, p<0.05), while human-in-the-loop output feedback was rated positively but only reached marginal significance (median = 4/5, p=0.07). From these findings we distill four design guidelines for explainable agentic AI. Our work also highlights open challenges for context-aware interactive debugging of multi-agent workflows.
