AB  - Context
: The rapid proliferation of Generative AI (GenAI) has created a trust crisis characterized by “black box” opacity and a usability paradox. While research focuses on technical alignment, the human factor–specifically how a lack of historical context contributes to automation bias and deskilling–remains under-scrutinized.
Objectives
: This study proposes the Digital Evolution Cartography (DEC) decision-support framework to quantify the relationship between historical computing literacy and trust calibration. We aim to demonstrate how historical pattern recognition serves as a cognitive mechanism to distinguish genuine capability from stochastic hallucination.
Methods
: We utilize a rigorous analysis of computing history over the past seven decades alongside open-access datasets to validate the DEC model. The methodology includes the development of the Historical Computing Knowledge Index (HCKI) to measure computing literacy and its association with user behavior. We defined a trust calibration function based on historical patterns to quantify the impact of historical knowledge on modern AI interactions.
Results
: Our findings reveal a strong logarithmic association between HCKI scores and Trust Calibration Accuracy (r = 0.72, 95% CI [0.66, 0.77], p < 0.001). Organizations with high HCKI scores exhibited significantly higher trust calibration (43% reduction in critical acceptance errors, Cohen's d = 0.87) and more responsible usage protocols compared to those with low historical literacy. The DEC decision-support framework demonstrated the ability to map historical failures (e.g., 1980s Expert Systems) to modern GenAI vulnerabilities (e.g., 2025 Agentic AI Loops) with high predictive accuracy.
Conclusion
: We argue that history is a critical interface for responsible AI adoption. The DEC decision-support framework treats history as a dynamic predictor of user behavior, and our findings suggest that high historical computing literacy is positively associated with mitigating the risks of modern Generative systems.
ER  - 

