AB  - In this paper, we propose an example of secure federated learning with the use of differential privacy and partial model sharing, evaluated by a Python-based simulation with the MNIST dataset. It achieves privacy preserving collaboration training of the decentralized neural network with up to 5 clients. Every client train on local noniid data, shares 60% selected model parameters, and adds Laplace noise for privacy protection. The central server accumulates these noisy updates to improve the global model with iterative. Across >30 federated rounds, we observe a consistent growth in both training and test accuracy, which confirms the efficacy of the framework. While having door swings and noise-related sources of discrepancy, the model retains it is the generalization capacity, with closely-located curves of accuracy. Although the training and testing losses were increasing probably because of noise accumulation, the model could still come to a reasonable convergence. This protocol-gap works well between efficiency (in terms of accuracy, privacy, and communication costs) and thus be useful in sensitive domains such as healthcare, finance and mobile applications. Our future work will enhance the mechanism with adaptive privacy and secure aggregation and integrate it with a deployment.
